"Since the problems of designing sparse filters were formulated in the same manner for the 1D and 2D cases, this section describes an optimization method that works in both situations. Since the maximization of the number of zero coefficients is NP-hard in general, at the center of our method stands the \(l_{1}\) minimization technique. We hope to obtain good results because the \(l_{1}\) minimization problem is the convex relaxation of the original problem which is discrete in nature and non-convex. In order to find high order filters that have a large number of zero coefficients and still manage to keep the error below a fixed value \(\delta^{\text{imposed}}\), we use a combination of the two basic approaches in the sparse representations field: reweighted \(l_{1}\) minimization and greedy algorithms. The general structure of the proposed algorithm, called IRLIG, is described in Table 1.

In Stage A of the proposed method, the IRL1 minimization problem

\[\underset{\mathbf{x}}{\text{minimize}} \mathbf{c}^{\intercal}\mathbf{x}+\mu\lvert\mathbf{W}\mathbf{x}\rvert_{1}\] subject to \[\mathbf{Q}\times\mathbf{s}\times\mathbf{b},\] \[x_{i}=0,\quad\forall i\in S,\] \[\mathbf{c}^{\intercal}\mathbf{x}\leq\delta^{\text{imposed}} \tag{10}\]

is solved; the \(l_{1}\) penalization term is added to the criterion of (3) or (9) in order to promote sparse solutions. The diagonal matrix \(\mathbf{W}\) contains the weights associated with each coefficient and is initially

\[\mathbf{W}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{I}&\mathbf{0}\\ \mathbf{0}&0\end{bmatrix}, \tag{11}\]

**Parameters**

1. maxSteps \(>1\), the maximum number of iterations for the IRL1 minimization
2. \(\epsilon,0<\varepsilon\ll 1\), parameter used for the computation of weights
3. \(\varepsilon^{\text{stop}},0<\varepsilon^{\text{stop}}\ll 1\), used as an extra stopping condition
4. \(\varepsilon^{\text{cut}},0<\varepsilon^{\text{cut}}\ll 1\), elimination threshold used to decide if a filter coefficient is permanently set to zero
5. \(a>1\), extra penalizing weight
6. \(S=0\), can be nonempty if prior information about the support is available

**Procedure** for step = 1 \(\cdots\) maxSteps do
7. solve problem (10)
8. if problem (10) is feasible \[\text{if}\lvert\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}^{\text{previous}}\rvert_{2}<\varepsilon^{\text{ stop}}\text{ then break for loop}\] (12)
9. if problem (10) is not feasible * return to previous solution \(\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{x}^{\text{previous}}\) and set \(S=\mathbf{x}^{\text{previous}}\) * decrease elimination threshold \(\varepsilon^{\text{cut}}=\varepsilon^{\text{cut}}/10\)
10. compute new weights \[W_{ii}=\begin{cases}\dfrac{a}{\lvert\mathbf{x}\rvert+\varepsilon}&\text{2D case, if }\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbf{\hat{H}}_{33}\\ \dfrac{1}{\lvert\mathbf{x}\rvert_{i}\rvert+\varepsilon}&\text{otherwise}\end{cases}\] (13)
11. decide the support of \(\mathbf{x}\) \[S=\{i\ \mid\ \lvert\mathbf{x}_{i}\rvert\leq\varepsilon^{\text{cut}}\}\] (14)"
